  "architecture": "Use the existing Next.js application as the backend. GET /api/status reports whether its handler can execute, plus the package.json release version, normalized application environment and request timestamp. Availability does not include Supabase readiness; configured and mock deployments use the same liveness contract. /status consists of a server-rendered shell and one client StatusPanel that requests the actual endpoint on mount and on manual refresh. This resolves the server-helper versus client-fetch disagreement in favor of observing the API transport. Shared response types belong in types/index.ts, metadata construction in lib/status.ts, and runtime environment reads in the route handler. A narrow proxy bypass prevents /api/status and /status from invoking updateSession; the page retains the existing layout and Navbar. Add one footer link. Reuse existing documentation instead of creating another document. No separate backend service, Supabase probe, degraded state, polling, custom environment variables, migrations, dependencies or infrastructure are needed for this scope.",
